<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Livadeia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lebadea, Lebadeia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Livadeia, ancient Lebadea, is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, today the capital of the Boeotia regional unit. In antiquity it was famous for the oracle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trophonius">Trophonius</a>, consulted by visitors who descended into an underground chamber, an experience described by Pausanias. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and under Ottoman rule it was an important regional centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/livadeia.jpg" alt="Livadeia" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: C messier · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%9B%CE%B9%CE%B2%CE%B1%CE%B4%CE%B5%CE%B9%CE%AC_1722-Pano.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
